1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,960
Jonah has been hearing a voice that other people cannot hear.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:08,385
Ordinary events feel unusually threatening, and daily tasks have

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,225
become harder. He needs someone to take his experience seriously

4
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,120
and help him feel safer.

5
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:16,960
Assessment is the beginning of that help.

6
00:00:17,448 --> 00:00:22,108
Schizophrenia can affect perception, beliefs, thinking, motivation

7
00:00:22,168 --> 00:00:25,748
and everyday functioning. It is not a split personality.

8
00:00:26,368 --> 00:00:30,073
Experiences vary greatly, and the diagnosis does not define the

9
00:00:30,088 --> 00:00:34,233
whole person. Understanding both symptoms and practical needs

10
00:00:34,248 --> 00:00:36,888
helps us build a useful treatment plan.

11
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:41,203
This is general information for adults, not medical advice.

12
00:00:41,783 --> 00:00:45,768
New or worsening psychotic experiences need prompt professional

13
00:00:45,783 --> 00:00:49,808
assessment. If someone is in immediate danger or cannot stay

14
00:00:49,823 --> 00:00:52,243
safe, seek urgent local help.

15
00:00:52,663 --> 00:00:56,663
Do not stop or change prescribed medication because of a video.

16
00:00:57,611 --> 00:01:01,516
Psychosis can include hearing voices, strongly held beliefs that

17
00:01:01,531 --> 00:01:04,851
others do not share, or difficulty organising thought.

18
00:01:05,231 --> 00:01:08,791
It can occur in several conditions, not only schizophrenia.

19
00:01:09,571 --> 00:01:13,696
Clinicians consider mood episodes, substances, medicines and

20
00:01:13,711 --> 00:01:18,091
physical or neurological illness, alongside the pattern over time.

21
00:01:18,569 --> 00:01:22,834
Some people also struggle to get started, express emotion, speak

22
00:01:22,849 --> 00:01:27,649
easily or concentrate. These difficulties are not simply laziness.

23
00:01:28,249 --> 00:01:31,954
Depression, continuing psychosis and medication effects can add

24
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:35,909
to them. A good assessment looks for contributors that can be treated.

25
00:01:36,835 --> 00:01:41,400
Inherited vulnerability is substantial. Many common genetic differences

26
00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:45,955
each have small effects; some rare changes have larger effects.

27
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:48,735
There is no single schizophrenia gene.

28
00:01:49,135 --> 00:01:52,740
Even people with very similar genetic inheritance can have different

29
00:01:52,755 --> 00:01:55,835
outcomes, so vulnerability is not destiny.

30
00:01:56,385 --> 00:02:00,225
Development matters, often before the first psychotic episode,

31
00:02:00,345 --> 00:02:03,745
but relevant brain processes continue into adulthood.

32
00:02:04,345 --> 00:02:08,585
Early vulnerability does not mean inevitable illness or deterioration.

33
00:02:09,525 --> 00:02:12,710
Researchers are still tracing how genetic differences affect

34
00:02:12,725 --> 00:02:16,465
brain cells, their connections and later experience.

35
00:02:17,024 --> 00:02:20,929
Adversity, social exclusion and some pregnancy or birth-related

36
00:02:20,944 --> 00:02:24,264
circumstances are associated with greater risk.

37
00:02:24,764 --> 00:02:28,229
Frequent use of strong cannabis probably contributes to psychosis

38
00:02:28,244 --> 00:02:32,849
in some people. Shared vulnerabilities also explain part of these

39
00:02:32,864 --> 00:02:37,104
links. No single exposure accounts for everyone's schizophrenia.

40
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:42,558
One of the strongest biological findings concerns dopamine, a

41
00:02:42,573 --> 00:02:46,598
chemical messenger. In many people with psychosis, parts of the

42
00:02:46,613 --> 00:02:48,753
brain produce and release more of it.

43
00:02:49,153 --> 00:02:52,998
This finding concerns particular systems, not too much dopamine

44
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:55,193
everywhere or in every person.

45
00:02:55,736 --> 00:02:59,801
One proposed explanation is that altered signalling makes ordinary

46
00:02:59,816 --> 00:03:04,081
events feel unusually significant. That may help explain how

47
00:03:04,096 --> 00:03:08,121
threatening interpretations develop. It remains a model, not

48
00:03:08,136 --> 00:03:09,896
a reading of someone's thoughts.

49
00:03:10,496 --> 00:03:15,256
Dopamine alone does not explain all difficulties with motivation or thinking.

50
00:03:15,799 --> 00:03:20,279
Other research examines how brain cells connect, coordinate activity

51
00:03:20,379 --> 00:03:22,919
and adjust their connections during development.

52
00:03:23,519 --> 00:03:25,739
Immune processes may also contribute.

53
00:03:26,239 --> 00:03:30,764
The idea that excessive removal of connections explains all schizophrenia

54
00:03:30,779 --> 00:03:36,359
is not established, and a routine scan cannot reveal one person's complete cause.

55
00:03:37,328 --> 00:03:40,593
Antipsychotic medicines are the best-established treatment for

56
00:03:40,608 --> 00:03:44,148
hallucinations, delusions and disorganised thinking.

57
00:03:44,568 --> 00:03:47,968
They can also reduce relapse when effective treatment continues.

58
00:03:48,548 --> 00:03:51,228
There is no universally best first medicine.

59
00:03:51,788 --> 00:03:55,793
Choice should consider likely benefits, previous response, side

60
00:03:55,808 --> 00:03:57,928
effects and the person's preferences.

61
00:03:58,521 --> 00:04:02,446
Some people prefer tablets. Others find a long-acting injection

62
00:04:02,461 --> 00:04:05,021
more convenient or easier to sustain.

63
00:04:05,581 --> 00:04:07,486
Both need a jointly agreed plan.

64
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:11,806
Follow-up should ask about sleepiness, restlessness, movement

65
00:04:11,821 --> 00:04:16,921
problems, weight gain and other effects, as well as whether symptoms are improving.

66
00:04:17,475 --> 00:04:21,535
If psychotic symptoms persist after two adequate medication trials,

67
00:04:21,815 --> 00:04:24,715
the team should assess whether clozapine is appropriate.

68
00:04:25,315 --> 00:04:28,520
First check that the earlier treatments had a fair trial and

69
00:04:28,535 --> 00:04:31,015
consider other reasons for poor response.

70
00:04:31,595 --> 00:04:35,435
Repeatedly switching standard medicines can delay useful care.

71
00:04:35,937 --> 00:04:39,702
Clozapine requires careful monitoring, including blood tests

72
00:04:39,717 --> 00:04:44,142
and attention to heart problems, severe constipation and metabolic

73
00:04:44,157 --> 00:04:48,282
health. Its recommended role is established, while estimates

74
00:04:48,297 --> 00:04:51,862
of its advantage over other newer medicines remain uncertain

75
00:04:51,877 --> 00:04:56,477
in some comparisons. Benefits and burdens both need discussion.

76
00:04:57,424 --> 00:05:01,069
Psychological treatment can help people cope with experiences

77
00:05:01,084 --> 00:05:05,984
and reduce distress. In cognitive behavioural therapy for psychosis,

78
00:05:06,404 --> 00:05:10,269
the person explores interpretations and tries practical coping

79
00:05:10,284 --> 00:05:14,829
strategies with a therapist. Average benefits are modest, and

80
00:05:14,844 --> 00:05:18,124
most evidence concerns therapy added to usual treatment.

81
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:23,525
Structured family support can explain experiences, improve communication

82
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:25,640
and help people plan for difficulties.

83
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:29,940
It can reduce the burden on relatives and may reduce relapse.

84
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:32,580
This is support for a shared challenge;

85
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,180
it does not mean the family caused the illness.

86
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:39,660
Early intervention teams can coordinate medication, therapy,

87
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,280
family work and help with education or employment.

88
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:47,705
Supported employment helps people who want a job seek ordinary

89
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:49,940
paid work with continuing support.

90
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:54,325
Training in attention and memory can also help, especially when

91
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:55,840
linked to everyday goals.

92
00:05:56,753 --> 00:05:58,833
Physical health belongs in the plan.

93
00:05:59,353 --> 00:06:02,793
That includes checking weight, blood pressure and blood sugar,

94
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:06,518
addressing smoking and making activity and ordinary health care

95
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:11,033
accessible. Persistent low motivation deserves its own review;

96
00:06:11,493 --> 00:06:15,853
simply increasing medication does not reliably solve every difficulty.

97
00:06:16,411 --> 00:06:20,096
Medication decisions should balance relapse protection with treatment

98
00:06:20,111 --> 00:06:23,936
burden. Some people may discuss carefully supported reduction

99
00:06:23,951 --> 00:06:27,796
with their clinician, but trials do not support routine early

100
00:06:27,811 --> 00:06:32,596
stopping. Any change needs an agreed plan, gradual adjustment

101
00:06:32,611 --> 00:06:35,771
when appropriate and monitoring for returning symptoms.

102
00:06:36,345 --> 00:06:40,150
For Jonah, progress may mean feeling less threatened, reconnecting

103
00:06:40,165 --> 00:06:43,045
with people and returning to activities he values.

104
00:06:43,665 --> 00:06:47,565
Recovery differs between people and can include continuing support.

105
00:06:48,065 --> 00:06:52,470
The aim is a safer, more satisfying life, shaped with the person

106
00:06:52,485 --> 00:06:54,645
rather than defined by the diagnosis.
